Wait — let me verify the sign of the 90° yaw in quatFromEuler. In the existing test: "yaw rotates the behind offset (90° → -X side)" for the on-foot case: with yaw=+90°, the character looks toward +X, so the camera sits on the -X side. And for the cockpit: "90° yaw: +Z forward becomes +X." So quatFromEuler(π/2, 0, 0) maps +Z → +X. Good, so for my chase test: behind local (0,4,-14) rotated by yaw90: local -Z → world -X. So position = (−14, 4, 0). Look: local +Z 20 → world +X: (20, 0, 0). ✓.

Also the SPAWN_GATE_QUAT test — the span length: |look - position| = |ahead_world - behind_world| = |quat*(0,0,20) - quat*(0,4,-14)| = |(0,-4,34)| = sqrt(16+1156) = sqrt(1172) ≈ 34.23. But CHASE_LOOK_AHEAD + CHASE_BEHIND = 34. These are NOT equal! The camera is offset in the +Y direction too, so the span is sqrt(4² + 34²). My assertion `toBeCloseTo(CHASE_LOOK_AHEAD + CHASE_BEHIND, 6)` will fail.

Let me fix it: the span length should be sqrt(CHASE_HEIGHT² + (BEHIND+LOOK_AHEAD)²). And toLook = quat*(0, -4, 34). Let me rewrite that test more carefully:

